hey this is presto Walker reminding you  to mind your decisions this comes from  an Oxford interview question you are  given a coin not necessarily fair that  sometimes shows heads and sometimes  shows tails if you toss a tails you get  one pound and the game continues so you  get to toss again if you toss a heads  however you get nothing and the game  ends the question is what is the average  amount you will win in the game in other  words what is the expected value of the  game I adapted this from a video I saw  on the YouTube channel Kurumi pause the  video if you'd like to give it a try and  when you're ready keep watching to learn  how to solve this problem  [Music]  now before we go calculating I'm going  to prove this series is absolutely  convergent this is so we can deal with  the infinite series and not get any  crazy results we'll write T to be the  probability of getting a tails H to be 1  minus T which is the probability of  getting a heads we'll write W to be a  random variable equal to the winnings of  the game we want to calculate e of W if  we toss a heads we'll get 0 if we toss a  tails and then a heads we'll get 1 if we  toss two tails and then a heads we'll  get 2 and this pattern continues so how  do we calculate this infinite series  well first substitute in the  probabilities of heads and tails we get  the following infinite sum now we're  going to prove that this is absolutely  convergent the general term of this  series is T to the power of K multiplied  by the quantity 1 minus T multiplied by  K starting at K is equal to 0 now we'll  use Dalembert ratio test of consecutive  terms  well then simplify this and we see what  happens when K goes to infinity this  limit will go to T and that's going to  be less than 1 because T is the  probability of getting a tails which is  strictly between 0 &amp; 1  thus this series is absolutely  convergent so now let's evaluate this  series how are we going to do that the  first thing we'll do is we'll distribute  the term 1 minus T now since we prove  this series is absolutely convergent we  can regroup the terms with no issues we  won't get a ridiculous result like the  sum of all natural numbers is equal to  negative 1/12  so we'll first group these T squared  terms then we will go ahead and do the  same thing and group the T cubed terms  then we'll continue to do that these T  squared terms will simplify to be T  squared the T cubed terms will simplify  to be T cubed and this pattern will  continue so we end up with a simple  geometric series and this is equal to T  all over 1 minus it's common ratio of T  and that's our answer  so I think this  is a pretty cool way to solve the  problem but there's another way that I  think is also interesting it uses the  law of total expectation for a finite  random variable X where we have a sample  space going from a 1 to a n it takes the  following formula we take the  conditioned expectation multiply it by  the probability and then add up all of  these terms now this formula looks very  complicated but it'll actually be useful  because these conditional expectations  are sometimes easier to calculate so  we'll condition on the first coin toss  when we start the game we expect to win  e of W then on the first toss we might  get a heads in that case we're going to  get 0 and the game is going to end but  we might also get a tails in which case  we'll get 1 pound the game is going to  restart and then we'll still continue to  expect a of W so to put it another way  you might throw a heads and get 0 or you  might throw a tails in which case you  win one pound and the game restarts so  you now expect to win a of W plus one so  I'll write this in a simple algebra  equation we first use the formula for  the law of total expectation and then we  substitute what we just calculated we  then simplify this equation and now we  have a linear equation in a of W we then  collect the terms of e of W and then  divide both sides and we get o